-- [Lewis Acids & Bases] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Boron trifluoride reacts with ammonia in the gas phase to form a single adduct: \(\text{BF}_3 + \text{NH}_3 \rightarrow \text{F}_3\text{B}\text{-}\text{NH}_3\). In the product, a new bond joins boron to nitrogen. Which statement correctly identifies the Lewis acid in this reaction and the structural feature responsible for that role?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{NH}_3\\) is the Lewis acid, because nitrogen bears the lone pair used to form the new bond"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{BF}_3\\) is the Lewis acid, because boron has an incomplete octet and an empty valence orbital"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{NH}_3\\) is the Lewis acid, because the electronegative nitrogen pulls electron density from boron"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{BF}_3\\) is the Lewis acid, because the three fluorine atoms make boron the most electronegative center"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.'' In the Lewis model, an acid is any species that accepts a pair of electrons to form a coordinate covalent bond, while a base is the species that donates that pair; identifying the acid therefore requires locating the species with a vacant orbital rather than reading off charges or electronegativities. (Choice B) is correct: boron in \(\text{BF}_3\) has only six valence electrons (three \(\text{B}\text{-}\text{F}\) bonds and no lone pair), so it has an empty valence orbital and can accept the lone pair on nitrogen, making \(\text{BF}_3\) the electron-pair acceptor and thus the Lewis acid. (Choice A) is incorrect: nitrogen in \(\text{NH}_3\) does hold the lone pair used to form the bond, but donating that pair is the defining behavior of a Lewis base, so this feature identifies \(\text{NH}_3\) as the base, not the acid. (Choice C) is incorrect: it also mislabels \(\text{NH}_3\) as the acid, and although nitrogen is more electronegative than boron, pulling electron density is not the same as accepting a lone pair into an empty orbital, and \(\text{NH}_3\) is in fact the donor. (Choice D) is incorrect: it reaches the right species but for a false reason—fluorine, not boron, is the electronegative element in \(\text{BF}_3\); boron acts as the acid because of its empty orbital and incomplete octet, not because it is electron-rich. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Lewis acid-base definition to a novel reactant pair and reason from each species'' electron structure to assign the acceptor role.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'ce64d613-c297-4b9c-87e6-899a504dc5d7';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ce64d613-c297-4b9c-87e6-899a504dc5d7';
