-- [Octet Rule & Exceptions] ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Sulfur forms the stable neutral molecule \(\text{SF}_6\), in which the central sulfur shares six bonding pairs (twelve electrons). A student wants to predict which other elements could serve as the central atom of an analogous stable neutral molecule \(\text{XF}_6\). For which of the following elements is such a molecule NOT expected to form?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Oxygen"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Selenium"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tellurium"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Xenon"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.'' Forming \(\text{XF}_6\) requires the central atom to hold six bonding pairs (twelve electrons), an expanded octet. Second-period elements (\(n=2\)) have only four valence orbitals (one \(2s\) and three \(2p\)) and can therefore accommodate a maximum of eight valence electrons, so they cannot be hypervalent; elements in the third period and beyond (\(n\ge 3\)) have additional accessible orbitals and can share more than four pairs. (Choice A) is correct: oxygen is a second-period element limited to four valence orbitals and eight electrons, so it cannot expand its octet to hold the twelve electrons an \(\text{XF}_6\) molecule requires — no stable neutral \(\text{OF}_6\) forms. (Choice B) is incorrect: selenium sits directly below sulfur in the fourth period, has orbitals available beyond an octet, and forms \(\text{SeF}_6\), so it can serve as the central atom. (Choice C) is incorrect: tellurium is a fifth-period element that likewise exceeds an octet and forms \(\text{TeF}_6\), so a stable molecule is expected. (Choice D) is incorrect: even though xenon is a noble gas, it is a fifth-period element with orbitals to expand its valence shell and forms hypervalent fluorides such as \(\text{XeF}_6\), so it can be the central atom. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that only elements in period 3 or higher can expand beyond an octet to a set of candidate central atoms, distinguishing them by period rather than by counting the electrons the stem already supplies.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'f38747a2-57a2-4094-aa0e-3bf37573f408';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'f38747a2-57a2-4094-aa0e-3bf37573f408';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('f38747a2-57a2-4094-aa0e-3bf37573f408', 'B', 'misconception', 'believes a period-3+ element cannot expand its octet'),
  ('f38747a2-57a2-4094-aa0e-3bf37573f408', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes only sulfur (the stem element) can be hypervalent'),
  ('f38747a2-57a2-4094-aa0e-3bf37573f408', 'D', 'misconception', 'believes a noble gas cannot form covalent hypervalent fluorides');

-- [Lewis Structures] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'The cyanate ion \(\text{OCN}^-\) can be drawn as a Lewis structure in which nitrogen is triple-bonded to carbon and carbon is single-bonded to oxygen: \([:\!\text{N}\!\equiv\!\text{C}\!-\!\ddot{\text{O}}\!:]^-\). In this structure the terminal oxygen carries three lone pairs and one single bond to carbon. What is the formal charge on that oxygen atom?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(0\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(-1\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(+1\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(-2\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.'' Formal charge is a bookkeeping tool calculated as the number of valence electrons on the neutral atom minus the nonbonding electrons minus the number of bonds to that atom: \(\text{FC} = V - N - B\). For the terminal oxygen in this cyanate structure, neutral oxygen has \(6\) valence electrons, the three lone pairs contribute \(6\) nonbonding electrons, and there is \(1\) single bond to carbon. (Choice B) is correct: applying the formula gives \(\text{FC} = 6 - 6 - 1 = -1\), which also matches the requirement that the formal charges over the whole ion must sum to the \(-1\) ionic charge (here N and C each carry \(0\)). (Choice A) is incorrect: a formal charge of \(0\) is what oxygen carries only when it has two lone pairs and two bonds (as in a neutral C=O), but here the single-bonded oxygen with three lone pairs has one extra nonbonding pair, making it electron-rich relative to neutral oxygen. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(+1\) reverses the sign of the bookkeeping — it would arise from subtracting valence electrons from the assigned electrons rather than the other way around, or from giving oxygen too few nonbonding electrons; oxygen here has more electrons than a neutral atom, so it must be negative, not positive. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(-2\) double-counts by treating the shared bonding pair as if both electrons belonged solely to oxygen (\(6 - 8 = -2\)); in formal-charge bookkeeping each atom is assigned only one electron from each bond, so the bond counts as \(1\), not \(2\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the formal-charge formula to a specified Lewis structure rather than recall a memorized value.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'de376b50-fa62-4c15-9798-bfd3ee16e7c0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'de376b50-fa62-4c15-9798-bfd3ee16e7c0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('de376b50-fa62-4c15-9798-bfd3ee16e7c0', 'A', 'misconception', 'assumes_neutral_default'),
  ('de376b50-fa62-4c15-9798-bfd3ee16e7c0', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'sign_reversal'),
  ('de376b50-fa62-4c15-9798-bfd3ee16e7c0', 'D', 'misconception', 'double_counts_bonding_pair');

-- [Octet Rule & Exceptions] ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student is classifying four neutral molecules by how many electrons surround the central atom in the preferred Lewis structure: \(\text{BF}_3\), \(\text{NH}_3\), \(\text{PCl}_5\), and \(\text{CO}_2\). In exactly one of these, the central atom is electron-deficient, holding fewer than eight electrons. Which molecule is it?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{NH}_3\\), because nitrogen forms only three single bonds to hydrogen and no fourth bond."}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{BF}_3\\), because boron forms three single bonds to fluorine and carries no lone pair."}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{PCl}_5\\), because phosphorus forms five single bonds and thus breaks the octet rule."}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{CO}_2\\), because each oxygen shares a double bond, leaving carbon short of an octet."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category ''Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.'' Exceptions to the octet rule fall into three textbook categories: electron-deficient central atoms (fewer than eight electrons, common for groups 2 and 13), hypervalent central atoms (more than eight, only possible for period-3 and heavier atoms), and odd-electron free radicals. Identifying the electron-deficient molecule requires counting the electrons around each central atom, not just noting that a structure looks unusual. (Choice B) is correct: in the preferred Lewis structure of \(\text{BF}_3\), boron forms three \(\text{B}-\text{F}\) single bonds and holds no lone pair, giving it only six electrons; experiment favors three single bonds over a double-bonded octet structure, so boron is genuinely electron-deficient, which is why \(\text{BF}_3\) acts as a strong Lewis acid. (Choice A) is incorrect: in \(\text{NH}_3\) nitrogen forms three \(\text{N}-\text{H}\) bonds and keeps one lone pair, which totals eight electrons, so nitrogen has a complete octet rather than a deficiency. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{PCl}_5\) does violate the octet rule, but phosphorus holds ten electrons across five \(\text{P}-\text{Cl}\) bonds, so it is hypervalent (more than eight), not electron-deficient. (Choice D) is incorrect: the two \(\text{C}=\text{O}\) double bonds give carbon four shared pairs, which is exactly eight electrons, so carbon satisfies the octet rather than falling short of it. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the electron-counting rules for octet exceptions to distinguish an electron-deficient central atom from hypervalent and complete-octet cases.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd735e1e6-5f13-4eb1-a1c6-96b145d24f8a';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd735e1e6-5f13-4eb1-a1c6-96b145d24f8a';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d735e1e6-5f13-4eb1-a1c6-96b145d24f8a', 'A', 'misconception', 'assumes fewer bonds than four means an incomplete octet, ignoring the lone pair'),
  ('d735e1e6-5f13-4eb1-a1c6-96b145d24f8a', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'conflates octet violation by expansion (hypervalent) with deficiency'),
  ('d735e1e6-5f13-4eb1-a1c6-96b145d24f8a', 'D', 'misconception', 'miscounts double-bond electrons as leaving carbon below an octet');

